(a) Generally. The procedure for review and approval of a subdivision plat consists of 2 separate steps. The initial step is the preparation and submission to the Planning Commission of a preliminary plat of the proposed subdivision. The second step is the preparation and submission to the Planning Commission of a final plat, together with required certificates. This final plat becomes the instrument to be recorded in the Office of the Clerk of the Superior Court of the County, when duly signed by the Chair of the Planning Commission. All final plats approved by the Planning Commission must be stamped and in recordable form under law.
(b) Pre-application. The subdivider is encouraged to consult early and informally with the Planning Commission to obtain advice and assistance before he begins to prepare the preliminary plat. This will enable him to become familiar with the County Thoroughfare Plan and other official plans which might affect the area.
(c) Preliminary plat approval.
(1) Prior to making any street improvement or installing any utility, the subdivider shall submit the complete subdivision plan for preliminary approval, although he may plan to construct only a portion of it initially.
(2) The application for preliminary plat approval, including 6 prints of complete subdivision plans, shall be submitted to the Secretary of the Planning Commission at least 15 days prior to the Planning Commission's regularly scheduled meeting on the third Thursday of each month.
(3) The Planning Commission, before its review, may transmit prints of the preliminary plat to each utility service, Health Department, Fire Department and any other agency for review and recommendations in relation to specific service problems. A print of the preliminary plat may also be transmitted to the Coosa Valley Regional Development Center for its review and recommendations.
(4) Prior to a decision on the preliminary plat, the Planning Commission shall afford a public hearing. Notice of the time and place of such hearing shall be sent by registered mail or certified mail to the names as they appear upon the subdivision plat. Notice of the public hearing shall be sent at least 5 days prior to the date of the hearing.
(5) The preliminary plat, which shall meet the minimum standards of design and the general requirements for the construction of public improvements set forth in these regulations, shall contain a vicinity sketch map, at a scale of 1 inch equals 800 feet, showing the following:
a. Name and location of the subdivision;
b. Names of the owner and designer;
c. North point, graphic scale and date;
d. Boundaries and approximate dimensions;
e. Amount of acreage to be subdivided; and
f. Major traffic arteries, utilities and community facilities (major shopping areas, schools, parks, hospitals, churches) which are pertinent to the proposed design.
(6) The preliminary plat of a subdivision shall be drawn at a scale of not more than 1 inch equals 100 feet, and shall show the following:
a. Name and location of the subdivision;
b. Names and addresses of the owners and persons to be notified of official action;
c. North point, graphic scale and date (north point shall be identified as magnetic, true or grid north);
d. Topography at 5-foot contour intervals (unless a closer interval is required by the Planning Commission);
e. Street plan, which includes the following:
1. Location of all existing and proposed streets within the subdivision and adjacent to it;
2. Widths and purpose of existing and proposed rights-of-way and easements;
3. Clear identification of right-of-way location and width for any street which is considered part of the sketch thoroughfare plan;
4. Street names which are subject to approval by the Planning Commission;
5. Typical cross section of proposed streets, showing natural and finished grades;
f. Blocks and lots, with dimensions shown for all lot lines;
g. Building setback line along each street;
h. Feasible connections to existing or proposed utility systems and any proposed improvements requested of the County. When such connections are not practical, all proposed individual water supply and/or sewage disposal systems shall meet the approval of the Health Department;
i. Plans of all proposed utility layouts, which include the following:
1. Preliminary plan of sanitary sewers (if applicable), with grade, pipe size and points of discharge or soil tests as specified by the Health Department (prepared by the owner's engineer, with his seal affixed);
2. Preliminary plan of the water supply system (if applicable), with pipe sizes and location of hydrants (prepared by a professional engineer, with his seal affixed);
3. Preliminary plan of the storm sewer system, with grade, pipe sizes and location of outlets, subject to review and approval by the County Road Superintendent (prepared by the owner's engineer, with his seal affixed);
j. Location and dimension of land to be dedicated or reserved for parks, open space or other public use;
k. Any portion of the land in the subdivision subject to periodic inundation by storm drainage, overflow or ponding;
l. Existing zoning classification of the subdivision and all contiguous land. If the proposed subdivision does not lie within the force and effect of the existing zoning ordinance, the preliminary plat shall be accompanied by a plan indicating the proposed use of the lots and a copy of the instrument to be used in establishing restrictions;
m. Names of adjacent subdivisions and the names and addresses of record owners of adjoining parcels of land as they appear on the current tax records;
n. In case of resubdivision, a copy of the existing plat, with the proposed resubdivision superimposed thereon.
(7) Within 30 days after the first regularly scheduled meeting of the Planning Commission which is held 15 or more days after the submission of the preliminary plat, the Planning Commission shall review the plat and indicate its approval, disapproval or approval subject to any required modifications, unless the applicant, for the Planning Commission's approval, waives this requirement and consents to the extension of such period. If the plat is disapproved or is approved subject to modifications, the reasons for such disapproval or the required modifications shall be indicated in writing on 2 copies of the plat and shall be entered upon the minutes of the Planning Commission.
(8) One copy of the preliminary plat shall be retained in the Planning Commission's files, and 1 copy shall be returned to the subdivider at the time of approval or disapproval, with the Planning Commission's stamp, signed by the secretary of the Planning Commission or another duly qualified member of the Commission, along with any required change or modification endorsed thereon. Failure of the Planning Commission to consider any preliminary plat within 30 days, or such period as extended, shall be considered as approval of the same as submitted.
(9) Approval of the preliminary plat by the Planning Commission shall not constitute acceptance of the final plat, except when the final plat is completed during the specified time in substantial accordance with the layout shown on the preliminary plat. Approval of the preliminary plat shall lapse unless a final plat, of all or part of the area shown on the preliminary plat, in substantial conformance therewith, is submitted within 12 months from the date of such approval, unless an extension of time is specifically applied for by the subdivider and expressly granted by the Planning Commission.
(d) Construction of improvements.
(1) After the preliminary plat has been approved, the subdivider shall elect 1 of the following courses of action:
a. Installation of all required improvements. Installation shall conform substantially to the preliminary plat as approved; and, if desired by the subdivider, it may constitute only that portion of the approved preliminary plat which he proposes to record for final plat; provided, however, that such portion conforms to all the requirements of these regulations;
b. Deposit of cash or posting of a surety bond with the County Board of Commissioners' office, in an amount sufficient to ensure the completion of all required improvements, as well as all other surety requirements as listed in section 22-407 (surety for completion of improvements).
(2) After the completion of either 1 of the previous alternatives and recording of the final plat, the subdivider may proceed with the sale of lots.
(e) Final plat approval.
(1) Final plat approval shall be obtained by the subdivider as the preliminary plat or portions of it are constructed or included in the surety bond. The final plat shall conform substantially to the preliminary plat as approved; and if submitted in portions, each portion shall conform to all requirements of these standards.
(2) At least 15 days prior to the meeting at which the final plat is to be considered, the subdivider shall submit 5 copies (black and white or blue line prints), together with any street profiles or other plans which may be required by the Planning Commission.
(3) Prior to a decision on the final plat, the Planning Commission shall afford a public hearing. Notice of the time and place of such hearing shall be sent by registered mail or certified mail to the names as they appear on the subdivision plat. Notice of the public hearing shall be sent at least 5 days prior to the date of the hearing.
(4) The plat shall be drawn to a scale of 1 inch equals 100 feet, on sheets not larger than 17 inches by 20 inches. When more than 1 sheet is required, an index sheet of the same size shall be filed as a key, showing the entire subdivision, with the sheets in alphabetical order.
(5) The final plat shall contain the following:
a. A vicinity sketch map, at a scale of 1 inch equals 800 feet, showing the site in relation to its surroundings. The map submitted with the preliminary plat may be used.
b. The final plat of the subdivision, including the following:
1. Name and location of the subdivision;
2. Name and address of the owner and person to be notified of official action;
3. North point, graphic scale and date (north point shall be identified as magnetic, true or grid north);
4. Location sketch map, showing the relationship of the subdivision to the surrounding area;
5. Plan and profile of all streets and names of all streets, roads, alleys and other rights-of-way;
6. Total number of lots, total acreage, and total length of new streets;
7. Location of all blocks and lot lines, with all block and lot numbers in numerical order;
8. Building setback lines along each street;
9. Sufficient data to determine readily and reproduce on the ground the location, bearing and length of every road line, boundary line, block line and building line, whether curved or straight, and including the radius, central angle and tangent distance, and the length of curve for the centerline of all curved roads and property lines which are not the boundary of the property lines being subdivided. The length of all dimensions shall be to the nearest one-hundredth of 1 foot, and bearings of all angles to the nearest 1 minute;
10. Location, dimensions and purpose of all easements;
11. Location and description of all monuments and iron pins;
12. Final plat of sanitary sewers (if applicable), with grade, pipe sizes and points of discharge (prepared by a certified professional engineer, with his seal affixed);
13. Final plat of the storm sewer system, with grade, pipe sizes and location of outlets (prepared by a certified professional engineer or certified surveyor, with his seal affixed);
14. Final plan of the water supply system (if applicable), which must be reviewed and approved by the Planning Office and the County Fire Department as to pipe sizes, location and type of hydrants and valves. A minimum pipe size for new and replacement installation shall be no less than 6 inches, but larger sizes of pipe may be required, based on review and recommendation of the Planning and Fire Departments;
15. Computations, showing closure of the tract boundary, which closure shall be 1 foot in 5,000 feet as a minimum.
(6) The following certifications shall be presented along with the final plat:
a. Certification by the subdivider, showing that the applicant is the legal owner of the land, and that he formally dedicates all streets, rights-of-way and any other sites for public use.
b. Certification by a registered land surveyor (seal affixed) of the accuracy of the survey and plat, and the placement of all required monuments.
c. Certification of approval by the Health Department of the water supply and sewage systems installed or proposed to be installed, stating that they fully meet the requirements of the Health Department's regulations.
d. Certification by the appropriate County authority that the subdivider has complied with 1 of the following alternatives:
1. Installed all improvements according to the requirements of these regulations, or
2. Deposited cash or posted a surety bond with the County Board of Commissioners in an amount sufficient to ensure completion of all required improvements.
e. Certificate of approval by the State Department of Transportation for projects abutting any County, Federal or State roadway.
f. Certification of approval, to be signed by the secretary of the Planning Commission.
g. Certification from the County Board of Commissioners indicating that sufficient bond has been posted.
(7) When the plat has been approved by the Planning Commission, 1 copy, with the approval of the Planning Commission certified thereon, shall be submitted for filing with the Clerk of the Superior Court within 10 days as the official plat of record. One copy containing all required certifications shall be retained by the Planning Commission and 1 copy of the original tracing shall be given to the subdivider for his records. The Planning Commission must consider a final plat within 30 days after its first regularly scheduled meeting which is held 15 or more days after the submission of the final plat. Failure of the Planning Commission to act on a final plat submission within these 30 days shall be considered as approval of the same. Acceptance of the plat must be in writing in the official minutes of the Planning Commission, and such shall be stated in writing on 2 copies of the plat. One such copy of the plat shall be retained in the Planning Commission's files and 1 copy shall be returned to the subdivider within 5 days of the disapproval.
(8) Approval of a final plat by the Planning Commission shall not constitute acceptance by the County Board of Commissioners of the dedication of any street or other public way or ground. After approval of the final plat and the construction of streets shown thereon, the Planning Commission may recommend to the County Board of Commissioners that he accept these streets as public roads and take over their perpetual maintenance.
(Code 2005, § 34-481; Ord. of 7-20-1994, § 9.11; Ord. of 4-25-2001)

